1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:07,730
Right. Because, you know, imagine you're in your ministry, and you announce what seems like a simple update, uh, like changing an application fee, for example.

2
00:00:07,770 --> 00:00:08,690
That happens all the time.

3
00:00:08,890 --> 00:00:12,310
Right. But then suddenly, your vendors quote you weeks of work.

4
00:00:13,050 --> 00:00:16,410
It becomes incredibly costly to change just this one single number.

5
00:00:17,370 --> 00:00:18,310
Why does that actually happen?

6
00:00:19,030 --> 00:00:23,270
Well, it happens because that old figure, um, it doesn't just live in one place.

7
00:00:23,550 --> 00:00:24,890
It is duplicated across

8
00:00:25,490 --> 00:00:27,710
all these different screens and forms.

9
00:00:28,330 --> 00:00:30,550
The data is... Well, it's entirely fragmented.

10
00:00:30,710 --> 00:00:31,030
Oh, I see.

11
00:00:31,310 --> 00:00:36,550
Yeah. So it's costly to change because at the very beginning, nobody asked who actually owns that fee.

12
00:00:37,430 --> 00:00:43,970
Right. So to solve this duplicated data problem, we obviously can't have, you know, each individual service goal writing its own rules.

13
00:00:43,990 --> 00:00:48,390
Mm, absolutely not. That's where you need what we call, uh, shared groundwork.

14
00:00:48,790 --> 00:00:58,590
Shared groundwork. Yeah. Okay. Yeah. Instead of each goal having its own isolated data, you build a single shared document, so the rules are written there once, and every service goal

15
00:00:58,630 --> 00:01:03,370
just reads from it. Got it. And, um, there are three specific kinds of things that live in this groundwork.

16
00:01:04,010 --> 00:01:05,470
Number one, you have states.

17
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:06,090
States,

18
00:01:06,730 --> 00:01:08,310
like stages of a process.

19
00:01:08,410 --> 00:01:09,950
Exactly, the stages a record goes through.

20
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:15,570
Then number two, settings. This is a value with a clear owner, uh, like an application fee.

21
00:01:15,850 --> 00:01:16,070
Okay.

22
00:01:16,750 --> 00:01:20,410
And number three, code lists. Basically, the standard choices offered.

23
00:01:20,710 --> 00:01:24,630
Okay, but, um, let me ask a director general level question here.

24
00:01:24,670 --> 00:01:24,750
Sure.

25
00:01:25,950 --> 00:01:32,870
If this shared groundwork is the ultimate source of truth, who actually takes responsibility for confirming these values?

26
00:01:32,970 --> 00:01:37,950
Right. Good question. To see how that ownership works, let's look at a fictional country, uh, Progressa.

27
00:01:38,430 --> 00:01:44,410
Okay, Progressa. Yeah. It's a fictional country, and let's look at their quality authority, PHEQA.

28
00:01:45,150 --> 00:01:50,650
Let's start with those settings. The PHEQA application fee is twelve hundred in Progressa's currency.

29
00:01:50,910 --> 00:01:56,390
Okay. But its owner is explicitly the finance officer, sourced directly from Article VII regulations.

30
00:01:56,870 --> 00:01:59,210
So new values just carry an effective date.

31
00:01:59,810 --> 00:02:03,410
Oh, wow. So it's tied to an actual person and a regulation.

32
00:02:03,530 --> 00:02:08,650
Exactly. Now, for the code list, Progressa offers three kinds of institutions on the menu.

33
00:02:08,990 --> 00:02:14,110
Uh, number one, university, number two, university college, and number three, technical institute.

34
00:02:14,590 --> 00:02:16,570
And every goal uses that exact list.

35
00:02:16,670 --> 00:02:20,570
Precisely. And for the states, there are three states a license can be in.

36
00:02:20,970 --> 00:02:25,090
Number one, granted, number two, suspended, and number three, canceled.

37
00:02:25,490 --> 00:02:26,410
Okay, that makes sense.

38
00:02:27,070 --> 00:02:31,270
And the registration officer makes these moves, but it's based entirely on the minister's decision.

39
00:02:31,690 --> 00:02:34,790
Right. But okay, let me push back with a hypothetical here.

40
00:02:35,370 --> 00:02:39,450
Go ahead. What happens if an analyst just, you know, ignores this?

41
00:02:39,870 --> 00:02:48,750
Like, they just hard code a fee, say, um, a thousand dollars from an old letter sitting at the registration desk directly into the draft goal for a provisional license.

42
00:02:49,310 --> 00:02:53,210
Well, that's exactly why systems become siloed and so costly to change.

43
00:02:53,650 --> 00:02:59,070
If the step doesn't read the shared setting from the groundwork, the system asks the applicant for the wrong fee.

44
00:02:59,690 --> 00:03:01,670
Right, a thousand dollars instead of twelve hundred.

45
00:03:02,050 --> 00:03:06,650
Exactly, and the finance officer only discovers the error when the payments don't match her schedule.

46
00:03:07,050 --> 00:03:08,150
Ooh, that's rough.

47
00:03:08,670 --> 00:03:14,130
Yeah, and fixing it means painstakingly tracking down every single fragmented goal that copied that figure.

48
00:03:14,790 --> 00:03:19,370
The rule is ask, do not invent. If a setting doesn't exist, you raise a question.

49
00:03:19,650 --> 00:03:20,750
You don't just type in a number.

50
00:03:21,170 --> 00:03:24,810
Right. A figure that lives only in one goal's text is an invention,

51
00:03:25,430 --> 00:03:28,990
um, however reasonable it looks. Ask who owns it and write it once.

52
00:03:29,490 --> 00:03:37,490
Wow. Okay. So the next time you review a draft in your own program, look closely at the figures, the periods, and the lists of choices.

